# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene. Luke 10 opens with a sending: “After these things the Lord appointed other seventy also, and sent them two and two before his face into every city and place, whither he himself would come” (10:1). They are told what to say in a city that receives them: “The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you” (10:9). “The seventy returned again with joy” (10:17), and “In that hour Jesus rejoiced in spirit” (10:21). He then “turned him unto his disciples, and said privately, Blessed are the eyes which see the things that ye see” (10:23). The next sentence begins, “And, behold, a certain lawyer stood up, and tempted him” (10:25). Luke does not name the place, and he does not say whether the lawyer came from the company around Jesus or from outside it.'))
A(('p', '2.2 The question and the answer. The lawyer says, “Master, what shall I do to inherit eternal life?” (10:25). Jesus does not answer with a statement. He asks, “What is written in the law? how readest thou?” (10:26). The lawyer answers from the law: “Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy strength, and with all thy mind; and thy neighbour as thyself” (10:27). Jesus says, “Thou hast answered right: this do, and thou shalt live” (10:28). Luke then gives the lawyer’s next words and his reason for them: “But he, willing to justify himself, said unto Jesus, And who is my neighbour?” (10:29). The story follows at once: “And Jesus answering said, A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho” (10:30).'))
A(('call', 'A Question Comes Before the Story',
   'The lawyer asks, “And who is my neighbour?” (Luke 10:29). Luke says he asked it “willing to justify himself,” and he says Jesus, “answering,” told the story. Luke does not say what the lawyer meant to justify, and nothing is added here to say it.'))
A(('p', '2.3 The scene before the chapter. Luke 9 closes with Jesus turning toward Jerusalem. He “sent messengers before his face” and “they went, and entered into a village of the Samaritans, to make ready for him. And they did not receive him, because his face was as though he would go to Jerusalem” (9:52–53). Luke then goes on to the sending of the seventy. Luke does not connect that village to the story told in 10:30–35, and the two are not joined here.'))
A(('p', '2.4 The prophetic context. Jesus’ ministry in Luke is a ministry in which He says He was sent to preach the kingdom. “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent” (4:43). He sent the Twelve “to preach the kingdom of God” (9:2) and the seventy to say that the kingdom of God is come nigh (10:9). The lawyer’s question and Jesus’ reply both turn on the law: “What is written in the law?” (10:26). The story is read as told in that ministry, to a man who asks about the law, and not as a saying that floats free of it.'))
A(('p', '2.5 Why Jesus tells it here. Luke gives the occasion in one line: a lawyer who asks, “And who is my neighbour?” (10:29). The story is Jesus’ answer to that question, and it is told after the exchange of 10:25–28. Luke does not say that the story answers the first question about eternal life, and nothing is added here to make it do so.'))
