# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The passage is read here in the order Jesus gives it. Nothing is rearranged, nothing is added from another parable, and no meaning is given to any part before the text gives it.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The man on the road (10:30). “And Jesus answering said, A certain man went down from Jerusalem to Jericho, and fell among thieves, which stripped him of his raiment, and wounded him, and departed, leaving him half dead.” The first sentence names a certain man, a road from Jerusalem to Jericho, a fall among thieves, a stripping, a wounding and a leaving half dead.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The priest (10:31). “And by chance there came down a certain priest that way: and when he saw him, he passed by on the other side.” The sequence has three steps. A certain priest comes down that way “by chance.” He sees the man. He passes by on the other side.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The Levite (10:32). “And likewise a Levite, when he was at the place, came and looked on him, and passed by on the other side.” A Levite comes to the place and looks on the man. He too passes by on the other side. Luke’s word for the Levite’s action begins with “likewise.”'))
A(('p', '3.4 The Samaritan (10:33–35). “But a certain Samaritan, as he journeyed, came where he was: and when he saw him, he had compassion on him, and went to him, and bound up his wounds, pouring in oil and wine, and set him on his own beast, and brought him to an inn, and took care of him. And on the morrow when he departed, he took out two pence, and gave them to the host, and said unto him, Take care of him; and whatsoever thou spendest more, when I come again, I will repay thee.” The story turns on “But.” A certain Samaritan comes where the man is and sees him. He has compassion. He goes to him, binds up his wounds, pours in oil and wine, sets him on his own beast, brings him to an inn and takes care of him. On the morrow he takes out two pence, gives them to the host and tells him to take care of the man, with a promise to repay whatever more is spent when he comes again.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The three who see the man, side by side. The priest, the Levite and the Samaritan each come to the man and each see him. The text records what each does next.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['', 'Priest (10:31)', 'Levite (10:32)', 'Samaritan (10:33–35)'],
    ['How he comes', '“by chance there came down … that way”', '“when he was at the place, came”', '“as he journeyed, came where he was”'],
    ['What he does at the sight', '“saw him”', '“looked on him”', '“saw him … had compassion on him”'],
    ['What he does next', '“passed by on the other side”', '“passed by on the other side”', '“went to him, and bound up his wounds”'],
    ['What follows', 'Nothing more is said of him.', 'Nothing more is said of him.', 'Oil and wine, his own beast, an inn, care, two pence and a promise.'],
], [0.19, 0.27, 0.27, 0.27]))
A(('p', '3.6 What the story says and does not say. The text says the man fell among thieves and was left half dead, that a priest and a Levite each saw him and passed by on the other side, and that a Samaritan saw him, had compassion, cared for him and paid for his keeping. It does not say why the man went down to Jericho, who he is, or what his nation was. It does not say why the priest and the Levite passed by, or where they were going. It does not say what became of the man after the host took him in, and it does not say whether the Samaritan returned.'))
A(('p', '3.7 What follows the story (10:36–37). Jesus asks a question, the lawyer answers, and Jesus gives a command. “Which now of these three, thinkest thou, was neighbour unto him that fell among the thieves? And he said, He that shewed mercy on him. Then said Jesus unto him, Go, and do thou likewise.” These are Jesus’ own words and the lawyer’s reply. Jesus’ words are set out in Section 4, where Jesus’ own words about the story are recorded, and they are not paraphrased here.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
A(('h1', S4))
A(('p', '4.1 What Jesus says after the story. Jesus gives no explanation of the story. He does not say what any part of it stands for. After the last line of the story He asks a question: “Which now of these three, thinkest thou, was neighbour unto him that fell among the thieves?” (Luke 10:36). The lawyer answers, “He that shewed mercy on him” (10:37). Jesus then says, “Go, and do thou likewise” (10:37). Everything Jesus says after the story is in those two sentences, and the table records them in His words.'))
A(('ctbl', '', [
    ['What Jesus says', 'Jesus’ words', 'Verse'],
    ['His question', '“Which now of these three, thinkest thou, was neighbour unto him that fell among the thieves?”', 'Luke 10:36'],
    ['The three He names in the question', '“these three”', 'Luke 10:36'],
    ['The one He names in the question', '“him that fell among the thieves”', 'Luke 10:36'],
    ['What the lawyer answers (not Jesus’ words)', '“He that shewed mercy on him”', 'Luke 10:37'],
    ['His command', '“Go, and do thou likewise”', 'Luke 10:37'],
], [0.28, 0.54, 0.18]))
A(('p', '4.2 What the lawyer says and what Jesus does not add. The lawyer’s answer is “He that shewed mercy on him.” He does not say the word Samaritan. Jesus does not repeat the lawyer’s answer, does not say whom it names, and does not say after the story, as He did at 10:28, that the lawyer has answered right. He gives the command, “Go, and do thou likewise.” Jesus does not state what “likewise” covers beyond the word.'))
A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name in the story. Jesus does not identify the certain man. Jesus does not identify the thieves. Jesus does not identify the road, Jerusalem or Jericho beyond the names of the places. Jesus does not identify the priest or the Levite beyond their offices as the text gives them, and He does not say why they passed by. Jesus does not identify the Samaritan beyond the word “Samaritan.” Jesus does not identify the oil, the wine, the beast, the inn, the host, the two pence or the “when I come again.” Jesus does not say that the Samaritan stands for any person or any people.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only “these three,” “him that fell among the thieves” and a command, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the man is a certain people, that the priest or the Levite stands for a company, that the Samaritan, the oil, the wine, the inn or the two pence is a hidden figure of something else is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What the text records of the man. Luke says “a certain man.” He does not say that the man was a Jew, or a Gentile, or of any nation. The story gives the man a place of departure and a place of destination and a state, half dead, and it gives nothing more. The Samaritan alone is named by his origin, and Jesus does not say what the man’s origin was.'))
A(('p', '4.5 The explanation stays with this story. Jesus’ words in 10:36–37 belong to this story. They are not used to explain the other parables of Luke, and no explanation from another parable is brought here.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparison itself. It repeats none of Jesus’ words from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the story.'))
A(('p', '5.1 What the story does with the question. The lawyer asks, “And who is my neighbour?” (10:29). Jesus tells a story and then asks a different question: “Which now of these three, thinkest thou, was neighbour unto him that fell among the thieves?” (10:36). The question the lawyer asked is about the one who is near to him. The question Jesus asks is about which of three was near to the man in need. The meaning of the comparison lies in that turn.'))
A(('pk', '5.2 What the comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Need is met by what is done.** Three come to the man and see him, and the story separates them by what each does next: two pass by on the other side and one goes to him.',
    '**Mercy does not stop at the first act.** The Samaritan has compassion, binds up wounds, pours in oil and wine, carries the man to an inn and cares for him. On the morrow he pays and promises to repay more.',
    '**Standing does not decide who acts.** The priest and the Levite are named by office and pass by. The one who acts is named by his origin.',
    '**The question is turned.** The one who asks who his neighbour is is asked which of three was neighbour. The command that follows, “Go, and do thou likewise,” is put to him.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearer in the story’s own setting is a lawyer who has just answered from the law and then asked, “willing to justify himself,” who his neighbour is. He was meant to understand, from the story, that being neighbour to one in need is shown in what is done. Jesus spoke to him in the ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43), the ministry in which the seventy were sent to say that the kingdom of God was come nigh (10:9).'))
A(('p', '5.4 The weight of the contrast. The story gives no reason for what the priest and the Levite do, and no reason for what the Samaritan does beyond “he had compassion on him.” The force of the contrast lies in the three actions set one beside another, and it is not carried further than that.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What this section does not do. It does not say who the man is, who the thieves are, who the priest, the Levite or the Samaritan is, or what the oil, the wine, the inn, the host or the two pence is. It does not turn compassion, going to a man, paying or promising to repay into steps by which a person is saved. It draws no meaning from any other parable into this story.'))

