A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The story of the Samaritan belongs to Luke 10. It is kept apart here from three neighbors in Luke.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 12:13–21', '“Master, speak to my brother, that he divide the inheritance with me”', 'The Rich Fool. A request from the crowd about an inheritance, and a story of a rich man and his barns. It has its own study, and neither story is read into the other.'],
    ['Luke 16:19–31', '“There was a certain rich man … And there was a certain beggar named Lazarus”', 'The Rich Man and Lazarus. A rich man and a beggar at his gate, told to the Pharisees in another chapter. It has its own study and its own setting.'],
    ['Luke 7:41–43', '“a certain creditor which had two debtors”', 'The Two Debtors. Told in a Pharisee’s house to a man named Simon, about a creditor and two debtors. It is not a story about a man in need on a road.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The Samaritan is told in answer to a lawyer’s question after the sending of the seventy (10:1–29). The Rich Fool is told after a man in the crowd asks about an inheritance (12:13). The Rich Man and Lazarus is told in a chapter where the Pharisees are named, “who were covetous” (16:14). The Two Debtors is told in the house of a Pharisee named Simon (7:36, 40). Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The Samaritan story has a certain man, thieves, a priest, a Levite, a Samaritan and a host. The Rich Fool has a rich man and God. The Rich Man and Lazarus has a rich man, a beggar, Abraham and five brethren. The Two Debtors has a creditor and two debtors. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The Samaritan moves from a man left half dead to three who see him, to one who goes to him and cares for him, and ends on a question and a command. The Rich Fool moves from a plentiful harvest to a night when a soul is required. The Rich Man and Lazarus moves from a gate to a great gulf. The Two Debtors moves from a debt forgiven to a question about love. None of those movements is read into the Samaritan.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, the Samaritan’s compassion and payment are not the forgiven debt of Luke 7:42, and neither passage explains the other. Second, the “certain man” who fell among thieves is not the “certain rich man” of 12:16 or 16:19, and no feature of one is given to the other. Third, “neighbour” in 10:29 and 10:36 is read in this story and not joined to any other saying in Luke.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks to a lawyer who asked what to do to inherit eternal life, answered from the law and then asked who his neighbour is (Luke 10:25–29). The setting is the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and in which the seventy were told to say that the kingdom of God was come nigh (10:9).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The story of the Samaritan was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Luke 10:25–37 speaks of the Body of Christ, and the passage is not read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The lawyer’s question about eternal life, the answer drawn from the law, Jesus’ words “this do, and thou shalt live,” and the command “Go, and do thou likewise” are spoken in Jesus’ earthly ministry to a lawyer under the law. They are not carried over as the way the Body is saved. Compassion, going to a man, binding wounds and paying are actions in a story, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this story: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). The story is not read as a universal picture of how any person is saved, and the Samaritan is not read as the Body’s identity or destiny.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the story shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of seeing.** Two see the man and pass by on the other side, and one sees him and has compassion. A reader learns not to look at need and keep going.',
    '**A lesson of acting.** The Samaritan goes to the man, binds his wounds and brings him to an inn. A reader learns that mercy is shown in what is done.',
    '**A lesson of carrying it through.** The care does not end at the roadside. He pays for the keeping and promises to repay more. A reader learns to see a kindness through.',
    '**A lesson of the second question.** The lawyer was “willing to justify himself” when he asked who his neighbour was. A reader learns to weigh his own reason for asking a question about duty.',
    '**A lesson of a command received.** Jesus says, “Go, and do thou likewise.” A reader learns to take the command as a command to act and not as a subject for discussion.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the man, the Samaritan or the host. They are principles drawn from what the story shows.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'A lawyer asks Jesus what he must do to inherit eternal life, answers from the law, and then asks who his neighbour is. Jesus tells of a certain man who went down from Jerusalem to Jericho and fell among thieves who left him half dead. A priest and a Levite each see him and pass by on the other side. A Samaritan sees him, has compassion, binds his wounds, brings him to an inn, takes care of him and pays for his keeping with a promise to repay more. Jesus then asks which of the three was neighbour to the man who fell among the thieves. The lawyer answers, “He that shewed mercy on him,” and Jesus says, “Go, and do thou likewise.” Jesus names nothing else in the story. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the man, the thieves, the priest, the Levite or the Samaritan is, or that makes compassion or payment a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a man on the road, thieves, a priest and a Levite who pass by on the other side, a Samaritan who has compassion and cares for him, and a question and a command from Jesus.',
    '**The meaning:** need is met by what is done, and the question of who is neighbour is turned into which of three was neighbour.',
    '**The boundaries:** Jesus names only “these three,” “him that fell among the thieves” and a command; the man, the thieves, the priest, the Levite, the Samaritan, the oil, the wine, the inn and the host are not identified.',
    '**The present lesson:** seeing, acting, carrying a kindness through, weighing why a question is asked and taking a command as a command, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

